export const KNOWLEDGE_BASE = "# Page: About Us (about.html)\n\nAbout Us | VillageServer Initiative\n Skip to content\n VillageServer Initiative\n Back to directory\n About Us\n Who we are and how we work\n VillageServer is a practical ministry initiative focused on offline access, simple training, and durable field tools — built to serve people already doing ministry, not to become the center of the story.\n VillageServer isn't a tech startup. It's a ministry initiative that builds practical, offline-first tools so missionaries and local leaders can carry gospel resources into communities that ordinary websites don't reach.\n Resources distributed to a remote community during a VillageServer field deployment.\n Who we are\n We're a small team with a simple belief: the technology should serve the mission, not become the center of it. Every decision — which device to support, which file format to use, how to organize a folder — gets made through one filter: will this work in a local leader's hands, in a place with no internet, under time pressure?\n We work alongside missionaries, sending churches, and field organizations who are already doing the hard work. Our job is to extend their reach, not replace their relationships.\n What we actually do\n We build and support the tools missionaries and churches need to bring resources into hard-to-reach places. That includes:\n Raspberry Pi offline libraries — a local Wi-Fi library any phone can join without data\n Solar-powered field kits — for off-grid deployments where grid power isn't available\n Projector and audio setups — for group teaching in churches, schools, and evening gatherings\n Phone-sharing guides — step-by-step transfer routes for every device combination\n Custom content libraries — Scripture, audio, films, and training materials in local languages\n Printable field handouts — PDFs for every process, in plain language, designed for non-technical users\n The communities VillageServer serves — places where ordinary websites don't reach.\n How we think about this work\n Five principles guide every kit we build and every guide we write:\n Listen to the field worker first Ask what they actually face before choosing any equipment. The right kit is shaped by the setting, not by what looks impressive.\n Choose the simplest tool A phone transfer is better than a full server kit if it gets the job done. Add complexity only when the mission requires it.\n Package everything clearly Labeled, documented, and repeatable by a non-technical person under pressure in the field.\n Test with real users Watch where people get stuck, not where the plan says they should succeed. The first field test always reveals something the plan missed.\n Improve before scaling Fix what's confusing, update the PDFs, simplify the process — before ordering more kits or sending more teams.\n The initiative does not replace local ministry. It equips it.\n Download the About Us PDF for board packets or donor introductions.\n Download About Us PDF\n Key points\n Small team focused on practical, field-tested gospel access tools\n We serve missionaries and local leaders — we don't replace their relationships\n Every tool decision starts with what actually works in non-technical hands under pressure\n Download PDF\n Printable and shareable offline.\n Mission Statement\n Programs and Services\n Ministry Resource Partners\n Rollout and Reach\n Questions? Need more info\n Eric's companion site has extended resources, setup instructions, and more — including the VillageServer OS download and additional field photos and testimonials.\n Visit the companion site →\n © 2026 VillageServer Initiative · Directory · Pamphlets · Built by ArkiTech-Sol\n\n---\n\n# Page: Get and Share Resources (access.html)\n\nGet and Share Resources | VillageServer Initiative\n Skip to content\n VillageServer Initiative\n Back to directory\n Get and Share Resources\n The best access path is the one they can repeat without you\n Resources can reach people through local Wi-Fi, microSD, USB, phone-to-phone transfer, or printed guides. Start with the simplest method and add tools only when they genuinely help.\n Getting a resource onto a phone is only half the job. The other half is making sure that person can access it again without data, and teach someone else to do the same. The access path matters less than whether the local person can repeat it independently.\n Field workers and community members reading and sharing resources together — the goal of every access path.\n The available access paths\n Each path has a right setting. The simplest one that works is always the right choice. Don't introduce complexity the local team can't maintain.\n Local Wi-Fi (Raspberry Pi) — best when many nearby phones need a library at once; phones join like any Wi-Fi network, no data required\n microSD or USB — best for copying large folders or moving files between devices without internet; insert, copy, done\n Phone to phone — best when one person already has the resource; uses AirDrop, Quick Share, or LocalSend depending on device types\n Printed steps — best for keeping the process repeatable after the team leaves; one page, one route, no tech required to use\n Walking someone through it the first time\n Choose one path for this moment Pick local Wi-Fi, phone sharing, microSD, USB, or printed guide based on what's available right now. Don't explain all the options — just pick the right one.\n Open one resource, not the whole library Start with a single Bible book, audio file, film, or PDF. Opening the whole library first overwhelms people and slows down the session.\n Save it where they can find it again Put the file in a folder they can locate without data — Downloads, a labeled subfolder, or the app they already use for music or documents.\n Share it to one nearby person Use AirDrop, Quick Share, LocalSend, USB, or microSD to move the file to a second device while the first person watches.\n Leave the matching handout Give the person the printed PDF for this access path so they can repeat the steps without you being there.\n Ask them to teach it to one more person Have the new user show another person how to access the same file before the session ends. That's when it becomes local knowledge.\n Download the Get and Share overview as a printable PDF for field briefings and local leader training.\n Download Get and Share PDF\n Key points\n Use the simplest access path that works — don't add complexity the local team can't maintain\n Start with one resource, not the whole library; save, then share\n The process works when the new user can teach someone else without help\n Download PDF\n Printable and shareable offline.\n Phone-Based Distribution\n Transfer Between Devices\n Sharing the Library\n Setup Guide\n Questions? Need more info\n Eric's companion site has extended resources, setup instructions, and more — including the VillageServer OS download and additional field photos and testimonials.\n Visit the companion site →\n © 2026 VillageServer Initiative · Directory · Pamphlets · Built by ArkiTech-Sol\n\n---\n\n# Page: Ministry Partners & Affiliates (affiliates.html)\n\nMinistry Partners & Affiliates | VillageServer Initiative\n Skip to content\n VillageServer Initiative\n Back to directory\n Ministry Partners & Affiliates\n Stronger together\n VillageServer is built on relationships — with field ministries, Scripture providers, and technology partners who multiply what any single initiative can do on its own. Click any partner below to learn more.\n Each partner fills a specific gap. Click a card to read about who they are, what they bring, and how VillageServer works with them.\n Project Bible Runners\n Field deployment partner & founding ministry\n Field Ministry\n ▾\n Project Bible Runners is the founding field ministry behind VillageServer. Founded by Jess Sweitzer, Bible Runners exists to get God's Word into the hands of people who have never held a Bible — whether that means hiking into a remote village, organizing community screenings, or equipping local leaders to carry the work forward long after the team leaves.\n Jess leads the field side: building relationships in communities, coordinating deployments, and making sure the technology the VillageServer team builds is actually useful to real people in real settings. Her feedback from the field directly shapes the kit design and training materials.\n What Bible Runners brings: trusted community relationships, field leadership, spiritual oversight, and the boots-on-the-ground experience that keeps VillageServer grounded in the actual mission — not just the technology.\n Read the mission →\n Facebook: Project Bible Runners →\n Jess Sweitzer\n Founder & Director Project Bible Runners\n DBS\n Digital Bible Society\n Scripture content libraries & digital resource network\n Content Partner\n ▾\n The Digital Bible Society provides the Scripture content that lives on every VillageServer language card. DBS has spent years building curated digital Bible libraries — text, audio, and video — in hundreds of languages, formatted specifically for offline devices.\n Rather than VillageServer building its own content library from scratch, we use DBS's pre-built ZIM packages which include full Bible text, dramatised audio recordings, and the Jesus Film Project video library in the relevant language. This means a field kit is ready to use the moment it arrives — the content is already loaded, licensed, and tested.\n What DBS brings: hundreds of offline-ready language libraries, established relationships with Bible translation organizations worldwide, and licensing that allows free distribution in field settings.\n Visit dbs.org →\n TS\n TechSoup\n Nonprofit technology discounts & hardware sourcing\n Technology Partner\n ▾\n TechSoup helps nonprofits access software, hardware, and technology services at dramatically reduced costs. For a mission-focused initiative like VillageServer, that means Raspberry Pi kits, microSD cards, solar hardware, and projector systems can be sourced at a fraction of retail price — keeping kit costs low enough that small churches and field organizations can afford them.\n TechSoup provides discounts on hardware through partner manufacturers, cloud credits, software licensing, and access to a nonprofit technology community that shares sourcing knowledge globally.\n What TechSoup brings: meaningful hardware cost reductions, a vetted nonprofit procurement process, and a network of technology partners that can support specific field needs as the initiative scales.\n Visit techsoup.org →\n Become a partner\n VillageServer is actively building its network of field ministries, content organizations, and technology supporters. If your organization brings something that strengthens the reach of God's Word in unreached communities, we want to hear from you.\n What you bring\n Field relationships, local language content, hardware capacity, training expertise, or mission-aligned technology.\n What you get\n Listed as an affiliate, access to kit-building resources, co-deployment opportunities, and the VillageServer partner network.\n Who qualifies\n Registered ministries, nonprofits, and mission organizations aligned with placing Scripture in unreached communities.\n Apply to partner →\n Download the Ministry Partners overview as a printable PDF for board packets and partner introductions.\n Download Partners PDF\n Questions? Need more info\n Eric's companion site has extended resources, setup instructions, and more — including the VillageServer OS download and additional field photos and testimonials.\n Visit the companion site →\n © 2026 VillageServer Initiative · Directory · Pamphlets · Built by ArkiTech-Sol\n\n---\n\n# Page: Content Libraries and Custom Ministry Resources (custom-libraries.html)\n\nContent Libraries and Custom Ministry Resources | VillageServer Initiative\n Skip to content\n VillageServer Initiative\n Back to directory\n Content Libraries and Custom Ministry Resources\n The equipment only matters because of what it carries\n The library can hold Scripture, audio, gospel films, children's resources, discipleship materials, and local training files — organized, offline, and easy to browse on any phone.\n The Raspberry Pi, the battery, the projector — none of it matters if the library is poorly organized or missing what the community actually needs. The content is the ministry payload. Everything else is just delivery infrastructure.\n A curated content library ready for offline deployment — Scripture, audio, films, and discipleship resources in one place.\n What goes in the library\n A well-built library is organized around how people actually use it in the field — not by file type, but by purpose. The goal is that anyone with a phone can find something useful in under a minute.\n Scripture — full Bible text in the local language, formatted for phone screens and readable without an app\n Audio Scripture — recorded Bible readings for communities where audio is more accessible than text\n Gospel films — the JESUS Film, short evangelism videos, and locally relevant media ready to play or project\n Children's resources — illustrated stories, audio programs, and videos appropriate for children's lessons and family use\n Discipleship materials — follow-up studies, devotional guides, and teaching resources for local church leaders\n Training PDFs — setup guides, transfer instructions, and field FAQ handouts that double as kit documentation\n Building a library that actually works in the field\n Start with language Identify the local language, reading level, and whether audio is more useful than text for this community. A library in the wrong language is useless.\n Choose core resources first Prioritize Scripture, audio Bible, one gospel film, and children's content before adding anything else. These are the files that get used most.\n Organize by use, not by type Use simple top-level folders: Read, Listen, Watch, Children, Training, and Share. People should be able to find what they want by browsing, not by searching.\n Test on the actual phones Open every file type on the kinds of phones people in this community carry. A PDF that won't open on an older Android isn't a resource — it's a dead file.\n Remove anything broken or confusing Delete duplicate, broken, or unclear files before deployment. A cluttered library is worse than a small one — it makes people give up looking.\n Document what's inside Keep a simple record of which resources are in each kit version so partners and local leaders know what changed and what to expect.\n The content library is the actual ministry payload. The best library is simple, local-language first, and completely usable offline.\n Download the Content Libraries overview as a printable PDF for kit planning and partner briefings.\n Download Content Libraries PDF\n Key points\n Language and format matter more than file quantity — a small, right library beats a large, wrong one\n Organize by use (Read, Listen, Watch, Children) so anyone can browse without help\n Test every file on the actual phones before deployment\n Download PDF\n Printable and shareable offline.\n Raspberry Pi System\n Phone-Based Distribution\n Ministry Resource Partners\n Printable Pamphlets\n Questions? Need more info\n Eric's companion site has extended resources, setup instructions, and more — including the VillageServer OS download and additional field photos and testimonials.\n Visit the companion site →\n © 2026 VillageServer Initiative · Directory · Pamphlets · Built by ArkiTech-Sol\n\n---\n\n# Page: Field FAQ (field-faq.html)\n\nField FAQ | VillageServer Initiative\n Skip to content\n VillageServer Initiative\n Back to directory\n Field FAQ\n Quick answers to the questions that come up every time\n The FAQ addresses the most common field questions in plain language — internet, apps, power, device compatibility, privacy, and what happens when things go wrong.\n When someone is confused in the field, give them the next action — not a lecture. Each answer below is short by design. If a longer explanation is needed, it belongs in a full guide, not a FAQ. Point to the handout, the cable, the folder, or the next step.\n A child engaging with resources in the field — the system is designed to work for anyone, not just the technically confident.\n Common questions and plain answers\n Does it need internet to work?\n No. The local library runs completely offline. Once the Raspberry Pi is powered on and a phone connects to its Wi-Fi, all content is available without any internet connection. The core library works in fully offline settings. Satellite, when used, is a separate receive-and-replay path for Free-to-Air broadcasts — not required for normal library access.\n Does every phone need a special app?\n No. Basic library access through the Raspberry Pi Wi-Fi just needs a browser — nothing extra to install. Some sharing methods (like LocalSend) use a free app for cross-device transfers, but for joining the VillageServer library, a standard browser is all that's needed.\n What happens if power fails mid-session?\n The session pauses — but files that were already downloaded to phones are still accessible offline. This is why the setup guide emphasizes downloading files to the phone during the session, not just streaming from the server. If the server goes off, the downloaded content stays.\n Can local leaders run this without tech skills?\n Yes — that's the design goal. The setup process is six steps. The printed guides use plain language, not technical terms. The system is intentionally simple enough for someone who has never managed a computer before. If a local leader struggles to follow the guide, the guide needs to improve — not the person.\n What if the Wi-Fi name doesn't appear?\n Give the Pi 90 seconds to fully boot before checking. If the network still doesn't appear: confirm the Pi is receiving power (check the LED indicator), try turning the phone's Wi-Fi off and back on, and check that the power source is stable. Most \"not appearing\" problems are power-related.\n What if a file won't open?\n Try a different file first to confirm the connection is working. If one file fails but others open, the specific file may be corrupted — delete and re-download it. If no files open, the problem is the connection or browser, not the content. Clear the browser cache and try again.\n How do we update the content?\n Content is updated before deployment or anywhere normal internet is available. In the field, a personal hotspot or local Wi-Fi can be used when practical. Satellite broadcasts are handled separately as received or recorded media, not as the normal Pi update path.\n How to handle confusion in the field\n Listen for the real blocker Is the problem power, Wi-Fi connection, file location, device type, language, or confidence? Identify the actual issue before suggesting a fix.\n Give the shortest accurate answer first One sentence. If the answer takes three sentences to explain, the person probably needs to see it done, not described.\n Show the next action on the actual device Point to the button, cable, Wi-Fi name, folder, or handout. Have them do the step themselves while you watch — not while you do it for them.\n Confirm on the device before moving on The answer doesn't count until the person has done the step and it worked. \"I understand\" is not the same as \"it opened.\"\n If multiple people ask the same question, fix the guide Repeated questions mean the current explanation isn't working. Update the PDF or printed handout so the answer is there the next time.\n Download the Field FAQ as a printable PDF — the single sheet to leave in every kit for quick reference in the field.\n Download Field FAQ PDF\n Key points\n The library works completely offline — no internet needed to browse or download content\n Most field problems are power or connection related, not software related\n If the same question comes up repeatedly, update the guide — not just the answer\n Download PDF\n Printable and shareable offline.\n Setup Guide\n Get and Share Resources\n Transfer Between Devices\n Rollout and Reach\n Questions? Need more info\n Eric's companion site has extended resources, setup instructions, and more — including the VillageServer OS download and additional field photos and testimonials.\n Visit the companion site →\n © 2026 VillageServer Initiative · Directory · Pamphlets · Built by ArkiTech-Sol\n\n---\n\n# Page: Setup Guide (guide.html)\n\nSetup Guide | VillageServer Initiative\n Skip to content\n VillageServer Initiative\n Back to directory\n Setup Guide\n Six steps from kit to first file\n Unpack, power on, connect, open the library, test one file, and pack it back the same way every time. The field setup works in six consistent steps that any local leader can follow and repeat without technical help.\n A setup guide is only useful if a local leader can follow it under pressure, without the original builder standing next to them. If it reads like an engineering manual, it's too complicated. This process takes under two minutes once someone has done it once.\n The VillageServer kit unpacked — every component visible and labeled before setup begins.\n The six-step setup\n Unpack the kit Lay out the case, server, cables, battery, printed guide, and any optional items (projector, satellite hardware) so everything is visible before anything is plugged in. If a cable is missing now, fix it now.\n Connect power Use wall power, a charged battery, or solar-supported power depending on the setting. The Raspberry Pi draws very little current — a standard battery pack runs it for hours.\n Start the server Power on the Raspberry Pi and wait about 60 seconds. The VillageServer Wi-Fi network will appear on nearby phones when it's ready. Don't rush this step.\n Join from a phone Go to Wi-Fi settings on a phone and connect to the VillageServer network. Open the library address shown on the included card, or wait for the browser prompt to appear automatically.\n Test one resource before opening to the group Open one file — a Bible chapter, an audio track, a film, or a PDF — and confirm it loads. Do this before anyone else sits down. A broken test is fixable; a broken demo is not.\n Pack it the same way every time Return every cable, adapter, card, and printed guide to the same labeled place in the case. Missing cables are the most common setup failure at the next session.\n Running through the setup checklist during a field readiness exercise before deployment.\n Minimum vs. optional setup\n The minimum setup is: power, server, one phone, and a confirmed test file. Everything else is optional and should only be added when the setting calls for it.\n Minimum — power, Raspberry Pi, one phone connected to the Wi-Fi, one file opened and confirmed\n Add projector and speaker — when the session needs to serve a full room or outdoor group\n Add solar or battery backup — when wall power is unavailable or sessions will run longer than the local grid supports\n Add satellite — only when a Free-to-Air broadcast receive-and-replay path is useful in that region\n The setup guide turns the kit from equipment into a repeatable process. If a local leader can't follow it alone, simplify the guide — not the mission.\n Download the Setup Guide as a printable PDF for field kits, training sessions, and local leader handoffs.\n Download Setup Guide PDF\n Key points\n Test one file before opening the session to the group — always\n Pack the kit the same way every time; missing cables cause the next setup to fail\n The minimum setup is power, server, and one confirmed working file\n Download PDF\n Printable and shareable offline.\n Raspberry Pi System\n Power Systems and Solar\n Projector and Audio\n Satellite Systems\n Questions? Need more info\n Eric's companion site has extended resources, setup instructions, and more — including the VillageServer OS download and additional field photos and testimonials.\n Visit the companion site →\n © 2026 VillageServer Initiative · Directory · Pamphlets · Built by ArkiTech-Sol\n\n---\n\n# Page: VillageServer Initiative — Sharing the Gospel with Today's Technology (index.html)\n\nVillageServer Initiative — Sharing the Gospel with Today's Technology\n Skip to content\n Counting down to launch\n -- Days\n -- Hrs\n -- Min\n -- Sec\n English\n Español / Spanish\n 中文 / Chinese\n हिन्दी / Hindi\n العربية / Arabic\n Français / French\n Português / Portuguese\n Русский / Russian\n বাংলা / Bengali\n Bahasa Indonesia / Indonesian\n اردو / Urdu\n Kiswahili / Swahili\n Tiếng Việt / Vietnamese\n Tagalog / Filipino\n नेपाली / Nepali\n سنڌي / Sindhi\n ☝ Click any component to explore\n VillageServer Initiative\n Sharing the Gospel with the help of today's existing technology.\n Placing God's Word within reach of every village — even where there is no internet, no power grid, and no trained technician.\n Matthew 24:14 · Project 42: 42% of the world has never heard that Jesus loves them\n Explore the Initiative\n Read the Mission →\n Our mission\n VillageServer places the complete Word of God — Bibles, teaching, and gospel films — into the hands of people with no internet, no power grid, and no technician, and makes sharing it simple enough that one villager can pass it to the next.\n Read the full mission →\n 42%\n of the world has never heard that Jesus loves them\n 7,000+\n languages with little or no Scripture access\n 5\n field configurations — from a single microSD card to satellite receive-and-replay\n How the reach multiplies\n One library. Five ways to reach further.\n The same offline VillageServer library scales from a single card in one hand to a satellite signal serving a whole region. Each step multiplies how many people the Word reaches.\n 1\n microSD card\n Reaches 1 person\n Load the full library — Bibles, teaching, and films — onto a single card. One card, one person, fully offline.\n How sharing works →\n 2\n Wi-Fi sharing hub\n Up to 5 phones\n A pocket hub broadcasts the card wirelessly, so several nearby phones can browse and download the same library at once.\n Phone distribution →\n 3\n Raspberry Pi VillageServer\n 20–40 people\n A low-power server hosts the whole library over its own Wi-Fi, serving a gathering of phones with no internet and no data.\n Field configurations →\n 4\n Projector & audio\n Hundreds at once\n Play gospel films and teaching to a whole village at a single showing — one screen, one crowd, one evening.\n Projector systems →\n 5\n Satellite receive-and-replay\n Thousands across a region\n A satellite feed refreshes and rebroadcasts the library across an entire region — reaching 150+ and scaling into the thousands.\n Satellite systems →\n The Initiative\n Mission Statement\n Explore the Initiative\n About Us\n Programs & Services\n Testimonials\n Photos & Visuals\n Ministry Partners\n Equipment & Funding Application\n Technology & Kits\n Raspberry Pi VillageServer\n Power & Solar Solutions\n Projector & Audio Systems\n Satellite Systems\n Field Configurations\n Content Libraries\n Field Operations\n Phone-Based Distribution\n Get & Share Resources\n Transfer Between Devices\n Sharing the Library\n Setup Guide\n Rollout & Reach\n Printable Resources\n Printable Pamphlets\n Field FAQ\n Companion Site\n Eric's companion site carries extended resources not found here — including additional ministry affiliates, the VillageServer OS download and setup instructions , and a larger gallery of field photos and testimonials.\n Visit Eric's companion site →\n Field kit configurations\n Satellite receive path\n Projector system\n Solar field kit\n Field test run\n The team setting up the kit.\n These photos and video show a practical test run: phones, solar power, the VillageServer case, and satellite hardware being checked before field use.\n Your browser does not support the test run video.\n Phone and case setup\n Solar power check\n Workshop assembly\n Satellite alignment\n Support the Mission\n Give to the General Fund\n One gift keeps VillageServers built, shipped, and running — and powers every campaign, not just one. Give once, or set up a recurring gift to keep the mission funded year-round.\n ♥ Give to the General Fund\n Donation link coming soon.\n Secure donation via Zeffy — 0% platform fees.\n Get in touch\n Contact Us\n Questions, partnership ideas, or a story to share? Send a message and our team will reply by email.\n Name\n Email *\n Message *\n Send message\n &times;\n Field Guide →\n © 2026 VillageServer Initiative · Directory · Pamphlets · Built by ArkiTech-Sol\n\n---\n\n# Page: Full Initiative Overview (initiative.html)\n\nFull Initiative Overview | VillageServer Initiative\n Skip to content\n VillageServer Initiative\n Back to directory\n Full Initiative Overview\n Everything in one place\n VillageServer combines offline libraries, phone sharing, local Wi-Fi, power, projection, and optional satellite into a single field model for gospel access in low-connectivity communities.\n VillageServer is a portable gospel technology initiative. It combines an offline library, local Wi-Fi, phone sharing, battery power, group projection, and optional satellite into one field model that missionaries and local leaders can deploy and teach without technical support.\n A village community in East Africa — the kind of setting VillageServer is designed for.\n The complete picture\n Most field technology tools try to solve one part of the problem. VillageServer addresses the whole chain: getting content onto a device, making it accessible offline, teaching locals to share it forward, and leaving printed backups when phones aren't enough.\n The initiative isn't one product — it's a set of access paths that can be combined based on what the field actually needs. A simple deployment might use only phone sharing and printed guides. A more complex one might include a Raspberry Pi server, solar power, a projector, and a satellite receive-and-replay path for Free-to-Air broadcasts.\n A VillageServer team preparing resources for a field deployment.\n How the parts fit together\n Every deployment follows the same chain, regardless of kit level:\n Start with the mission context Understand the language, device types, power reality, and group size before choosing a single piece of equipment.\n Choose the right kit level Basic phone kit, Raspberry Pi library, power and media kit, or satellite-enabled — matched to the actual field setting.\n Load the library Scripture, audio, films, children's resources, discipleship materials, and local training files in a clear, browsable structure.\n Set up and train Unpack, power on, test one file, and teach one local person to run the whole process without help.\n Pilot before scaling Run the process in one trusted location, record every blocker, and improve before sending more kits.\n Report reach honestly People trained, devices reached, groups served, and resources shared — simple, honest, verifiable numbers.\n What makes this different\n No mobile data required — the library works completely offline at the point of use\n Local leaders can maintain it — the system doesn't require the original builder to be present\n Designed to multiply — one trained person teaches the next; the chain keeps going after the team leaves\n Printed backups — every process has a PDF so the work continues when phones die or servers are packed away\n Funding opportunities for Christian missionary organizations\n VillageServer Initiative actively supports Christian missionary organizations seeking funding for gospel-access technology deployments. Whether you are planning a first field deployment or scaling an existing program, the following pathways are available:\n Equipment and kit grants — apply for sponsored Raspberry Pi kits, solar power packs, and content library pre-loads through our equipment application program\n Partner organization funding — connect with partner organizations including Digital Bible Society and TechSoup, which offer technology funding and discounted equipment for qualified Christian ministries\n Campaign-based support — submit a deployment campaign request to receive field-specific guidance and coordinated fundraising support for your mission context\n Donor matching — qualified missionary organizations may be eligible for donor matching through ministry partners when deploying verified VillageServer kits\n If you represent a Christian missionary organization looking for funding to place offline Scripture libraries, gospel media, or VillageServer kits in the field, submit an equipment and funding application or connect with a ministry partner .\n Download the full initiative overview as a printable PDF for board meetings or partner introductions.\n Download Initiative Overview PDF\n Key points\n VillageServer addresses the whole access chain — not just one part of the problem\n The kit scales from a basic phone-sharing guide to a satellite-enabled field station\n The system is designed to work after the visiting team leaves\n Download PDF\n Printable and shareable offline.\n Mission Statement\n Programs and Services\n Field Configurations\n Rollout and Reach\n Questions? Need more info\n Eric's companion site has extended resources, setup instructions, and more — including the VillageServer OS download and additional field photos and testimonials.\n Visit the companion site →\n © 2026 VillageServer Initiative · Directory · Pamphlets · Built by ArkiTech-Sol\n\n---\n\n# Page: Field Configurations (kit-levels.html)\n\nField Configurations | VillageServer Initiative\n Skip to content\n VillageServer Initiative\n Back to directory\n Field Configurations\n Start with what the field actually needs\n From simple phone sharing to a satellite receive-and-replay setup — choose the configuration that fits the field setting and mission need. Start small, then add only what the place actually needs.\n There's no single right kit. The right kit is the one that solves the actual field problem — no more, no less. Start with the smallest level that works, and build from there only when the mission setting calls for it.\n A packed VillageServer field kit case — the foundation of every deployment.\n The four common field configurations\n These are not products for sale. They are planning patterns a ministry team can use when sourcing hardware, preparing content, and training local leaders. Start with Configuration 1 and add components only when the setting calls for them.\n Configuration 1 — Phone sharing — printed transfer guides, microSD card or USB drive loaded with resources, and simple step-by-step instructions. No technical setup needed.\n Configuration 2 — Local Wi-Fi library — Raspberry Pi, storage, and power supply. Creates a local Wi-Fi network that any nearby phone can join and browse offline.\n Configuration 3 — Power and group media — adds battery backup, solar panel, Bluetooth speaker, and a portable projector for group teaching sessions and film nights.\n Configuration 4 — Satellite receive and replay — optional Free-to-Air broadcast reception, projection, and replay path for regions where useful Christian content is already broadcasting.\n The Raspberry Pi server — the heart of the local Wi-Fi library configuration.\n How to choose the right level\n Define the use case first Is the need personal phone sharing, group teaching, local library access, projector teaching, satellite broadcast reception, or all of those? The use case drives everything else.\n Start with the simplest configuration Phone sharing costs very little and works almost everywhere. Start there unless you already know the field needs a local Wi-Fi library or group media setup.\n Add power only when it's a real need Choose battery or solar based on how long sessions run and how reliable the local grid is. Don't add solar if wall power is available.\n Add media for group settings A projector and speaker only make sense when one screen needs to serve a room or outdoor gathering. Add them when the kit is going to a church, school, or village setting.\n Assess satellite separately Dish, LNB, alignment, recorder, and power needs vary by region. Treat satellite as a receive-and-replay path that gets its own assessment — not a default add-on.\n Keep components itemized For board reporting and partner transparency, list each component — Pi, storage, projector, battery, solar, satellite receiver path — so the plan is clear and locally replaceable.\n The most common mistake is over-building the first kit. A simple phone-sharing guide and a loaded microSD card can serve a community for years.\n Download the Field Configurations overview as a printable PDF for board packets and field planning.\n Download Field Configurations PDF\n Key points\n Start with the smallest kit that solves the actual field problem\n Add power, projection, or satellite only when the setting calls for it\n Keep components itemized for board transparency\n Download PDF\n Printable and shareable offline.\n Programs and Services\n Raspberry Pi System\n Power Systems and Solar\n Satellite Systems\n Questions? Need more info\n Eric's companion site has extended resources, setup instructions, and more — including the VillageServer OS download and additional field photos and testimonials.\n Visit the companion site →\n © 2026 VillageServer Initiative · Directory · Pamphlets · Built by ArkiTech-Sol\n\n---\n\n# Page: Mission Statement (mission.html)\n\nMission Statement | VillageServer Initiative\n Skip to content\n VillageServer Initiative\n Back to directory\n Mission Statement\n Why VillageServer exists\n Helping missionaries and local leaders carry Scripture, gospel media, and practical ministry resources into places where internet, electricity, and local-language teaching are limited.\n There are communities all over the world with willing leaders, hungry hearts, and phones in their pockets — but no reliable way to access Scripture, training, or gospel media. VillageServer is the answer to that gap.\n From a Project Bible Runners run in Uganda.\n The problem we're solving\n Most of the world's gospel access challenges aren't about content — the Bible exists in thousands of languages. The challenge is delivery. Mobile data is expensive or unavailable. Internet connections cut out. Local-language resources don't surface easily online. And when teams go home, the access goes with them.\n VillageServer addresses that delivery problem directly. We help missionaries and local leaders carry a portable, offline library into any setting — and leave a system that keeps working after they leave.\n From a Project Bible Runners run in Uganda.\n What that looks like in practice\n Every deployment starts with a conversation, not a kit. We ask what the field worker actually faces — language, device type, power access, group size — and match the approach to that reality. Then:\n Choose the simplest path Start with phone sharing or a microSD card. Add a Raspberry Pi library, projector, or satellite only when the setting requires it.\n Load trusted resources Bible text, audio Scripture, gospel films, children's content, and discipleship materials — in the right language, in a folder structure anyone can browse.\n Teach one repeatable action Show one person how to connect, save, and share one resource. That person becomes the local trainer for everyone who comes after.\n Leave the system behind The kit stays. The printed guides stay. The local leader knows the steps. The work continues after the team is gone.\n Report what worked Write down what people opened, what confused them, and what should change before the next deployment.\n What we carry\n Scripture — Bible text and audio in local languages\n Gospel media — Films, teaching clips, and outreach materials\n Children's resources — Age-appropriate audio, video, and illustrated content\n Discipleship materials — Study guides, devotionals, and training PDFs\n Field training — Setup guides, FAQ sheets, and printed handouts for local leaders\n VillageServer is not a gadget project. It is a practical gospel-access tool for communities ordinary websites cannot reach.\n Download the Mission Statement as a printable PDF for board packets, donor meetings, or field briefings.\n Download Mission Statement PDF\n Key points\n The problem is delivery, not content — the Bible exists in most languages but can't reach low-connectivity communities\n The system is designed to keep working after the visiting team leaves\n Every tool choice starts with what the field worker actually faces\n Download PDF\n Printable and shareable offline.\n About Us\n Full Initiative Overview\n Programs and Services\n Rollout and Reach\n Questions? Need more info\n Eric's companion site has extended resources, setup instructions, and more — including the VillageServer OS download and additional field photos and testimonials.\n Visit the companion site →\n © 2026 VillageServer Initiative · Directory · Pamphlets · Built by ArkiTech-Sol\n\n---\n\n# Page: Printable Pamphlets and Field FAQ (pamphlets.html)\n\nPrintable Pamphlets and Field FAQ | VillageServer Initiative\n Skip to content\n VillageServer Initiative\n Back to directory\n Printable Pamphlets and Field FAQ\n The process that works when the team isn't there\n A PDF in a field worker's hand keeps the process going long after the team has left. Every route, every setup step, and every FAQ has a printable handout — designed for the field, not for a tech manual.\n Handouts should be short enough to use under field pressure. One route, one page, one clear set of steps. If someone needs to read three pages to understand the transfer process, the handout hasn't done its job yet. Every PDF in this library was written for a local leader who needs to work through it alone, in the field, on a phone or a printed sheet.\n Printed pamphlets and field kits staged for a deployment — each handout is one route, one page, ready to use.\n How to use the PDF library\n Pick the right handout before you leave Match the PDF to the field question: mission overview for a board introduction, phone transfer sheet for a specific device route, setup guide for kit deployment, or FAQ for quick answers in the field.\n Download before you lose internet access Save PDFs to the phone, laptop, USB drive, and microSD card before departing for the field. Don't assume you'll have connectivity to download later.\n Print only what you'll actually use Keep field packets light. A stack of twenty PDFs is overwhelming; five relevant ones get used. Bring the sheets that match the specific mission setting.\n Translate when the setting requires it If a local language version is needed, keep the same structure and simpler words. The step numbers and flow should stay identical so the process is recognizable even if the language changes.\n Store printed copies with the kit Every VillageServer kit should include a small folder with the relevant printed handouts. Keep digital copies on the device itself so they're available without the paper.\n Full PDF library\n Download any PDF below to your phone, laptop, or USB drive before heading into the field. All files are designed for printing and offline use.\n Loading…\n Key points\n Download PDFs before leaving internet access — don't count on connectivity in the field\n Keep field packets light: bring the five sheets that match this specific setting, not all twenty\n Store printed copies with the kit so they're always where they're needed\n Download PDF\n Printable and shareable offline.\n Field FAQ\n Transfer Between Devices\n Get and Share Resources\n Mission Statement\n Questions? Need more info\n Eric's companion site has extended resources, setup instructions, and more — including the VillageServer OS download and additional field photos and testimonials.\n Visit the companion site →\n © 2026 VillageServer Initiative · Directory · Pamphlets · Built by ArkiTech-Sol\n\n---\n\n# Page: Phone-Based Gospel Distribution (phone-distribution.html)\n\nPhone-Based Gospel Distribution | VillageServer Initiative\n Skip to content\n VillageServer Initiative\n Back to directory\n Phone-Based Gospel Distribution\n One phone shares to the next, no data needed\n Once one phone has a resource, it can move to another using tools people already know — AirDrop, Quick Share, LocalSend, USB, or microSD. No data plan. No technical setup.\n For many people in low-connectivity communities, the phone is everything — the classroom, the church, the study group. The question isn't whether they have a phone. Most do. The question is: how do we get the right resource onto it, and then make sure it keeps moving?\n Phone-to-phone resource sharing during a field distribution session — no internet required at any step.\n The right route for the right phones\n The sharing method depends on what devices are in front of you. Pick the simplest route that works for the two phones, confirm one file transfers cleanly, then teach the new person to repeat it.\n iPhone to iPhone — use AirDrop; both devices need Bluetooth and Wi-Fi on, and the receiving phone should be set to receive from everyone temporarily\n Android to Android — use Quick Share when both phones support it; use LocalSend when they're on the same local network\n iPhone and Android together — use LocalSend on a shared local network, or USB through a computer, or a microSD card with the right adapter\n Any phone from a microSD — insert the card, copy the files to local storage, confirm they open, then remove the card\n Running the transfer\n Save one resource on the first phone Download or copy one Bible, audio file, film, or PDF onto the source phone. Confirm it opens before starting the transfer.\n Identify both phones Ask whether each phone is an iPhone or Android. This determines which sharing method to use — don't guess.\n Choose the route and set it up Open AirDrop, Quick Share, or LocalSend on both devices. For microSD or USB, connect the hardware first, then open the file manager.\n Send one small test file first Transfer a single PDF or short audio clip before sending the full library. Confirm it landed and opens on the receiving phone before continuing.\n Confirm the file is saved and findable Have the receiving person show you where the file was saved — Downloads, Files, or a specific folder. If they can't find it, it won't get used.\n Ask them to share it to one more person The method becomes local knowledge when the receiving person does the next transfer themselves. Don't skip this step.\n Save one useful resource first. Then teach the person how to pass that one resource to someone nearby. That's the whole process.\n Download the Phone Distribution overview as a printable PDF for field briefings and training sessions.\n Download Phone Distribution PDF\n Key points\n Ask what type of phones are in the room before choosing a transfer method\n Send one small test file first; confirm it opened before sending anything larger\n The session isn't done until the new person has shared it to someone else\n Download PDF\n Printable and shareable offline.\n Transfer Between Devices\n Get and Share Resources\n Sharing the Library\n Printable Pamphlets\n Questions? Need more info\n Eric's companion site has extended resources, setup instructions, and more — including the VillageServer OS download and additional field photos and testimonials.\n Visit the companion site →\n © 2026 VillageServer Initiative · Directory · Pamphlets · Built by ArkiTech-Sol\n\n---\n\n# Page: Power Systems and Solar Solutions (power.html)\n\nPower Systems and Solar Solutions | VillageServer Initiative\n Skip to content\n VillageServer Initiative\n Back to directory\n Power Systems and Solar Solutions\n Keeping the kit alive in the field\n Power is the first question you answer in every field setting. Not the most exciting one — but the one that determines whether everything else works.\n Every VillageServer kit needs reliable power. That can come from a wall outlet, a charged battery, a solar suitcase, or a combination — the goal is enough runtime for a full field session, with a backup plan when the first option fails.\n Solar panel setup supporting a VillageServer deployment where grid power isn't available.\n The power reality in the field\n Many communities VillageServer serves have unreliable grid power, no grid power at all, or expensive generator fuel. A good power plan doesn't ignore that — it builds around it. The library is useless if the server goes dark in the middle of a training session.\n The good news: the Raspberry Pi and most VillageServer equipment run on very modest power. A standard high-capacity battery pack can run the server for a full day of use. Solar tops up the battery between sessions. Wall power is the simplest option when it's available.\n Testing solar power with the VillageServer case during the field readiness exercise.\n Your power options\n Wall power — the simplest option when reliable; always add surge protection and label cables clearly\n Battery backup — essential for outages, movement between locations, and pre-session charging before grid power is available\n Solar suitcase — for off-grid settings; needs full sun, careful placement, and disciplined pre-session charging\n Generator support — useful for extended sessions but adds fuel cost, noise, and logistics\n Hybrid approach — charge the battery from solar during the day, run the session from battery in the evening\n Planning power for a session\n Ask how long the session needs to run Calculate hours needed plus a buffer for setup and troubleshooting. A three-hour session needs at least four hours of power capacity.\n Use local power when it's reliable Plug into wall power with labeled cables and a surge protector. This is always the easiest path when available.\n Charge the battery fully before the session Don't assume the battery arrived charged. Check it the night before. A partially charged battery running a projector, server, and speaker is a short session.\n Place solar panels in full sun Even partial shade significantly reduces output. Check placement in the morning and recheck in the afternoon as shadows move.\n Test before the group arrives Power on the server, confirm the Wi-Fi network appears, and check that the battery shows it's charging before people start connecting.\n Pack the same way every time Return cables, adapters, solar panels, and the battery to the same labeled places in the case. Missing cables are the most common power problem.\n The most common reason a field session fails isn't the content or the server. It's the power plan.\n Download the Power Systems overview as a printable PDF for field briefings and kit planning.\n Download Power Systems PDF\n Key points\n The Pi draws little power — a standard battery runs it for hours\n Charge the battery fully before the session; don't assume it arrived ready\n Solar needs full sun and advance charging — it's not instant\n Download PDF\n Printable and shareable offline.\n Raspberry Pi System\n Field Configurations\n Setup Guide\n Projector and Audio\n Questions? Need more info\n Eric's companion site has extended resources, setup instructions, and more — including the VillageServer OS download and additional field photos and testimonials.\n Visit the companion site →\n © 2026 VillageServer Initiative · Directory · Pamphlets · Built by ArkiTech-Sol\n\n---\n\n# Page: Programs and Services (programs.html)\n\nPrograms and Services | VillageServer Initiative\n Skip to content\n VillageServer Initiative\n Back to directory\n Programs and Services\n A set of access paths, not one product\n VillageServer is organized around practical access paths — phones, local Wi-Fi libraries, power, group projection, satellite broadcast reception, and training materials — combined based on what the field actually needs.\n The initiative isn't one product. It's a set of practical access paths that can be combined or deployed individually depending on the field setting, available budget, and ministry context. Every path ends with the same goal: a local person who can use and pass on the resource without help.\n Kit components and logistics materials staged for a field deployment briefing.\n What the initiative actually does\n Each program area addresses a specific part of the access problem. They are designed to work together but can also function independently when time, budget, or field conditions require it.\n Phone-based distribution — moving resources from one device to another using AirDrop, Quick Share, LocalSend, USB, or microSD without mobile data\n VillageServer kit — a Raspberry Pi that broadcasts a private local Wi-Fi network so multiple phones can browse and download an offline library at once\n Power and media support — battery backup, solar panels, portable projectors, and Bluetooth speakers for group teaching sessions and film nights\n Satellite broadcast reception — optional receive-and-replay access to Free-to-Air Christian radio or video already broadcasting over a region\n Printed field guides — PDF handouts for every process, so the work continues after the visiting team has left\n Partner resources — coordinated content and ministry support through trusted affiliates including Digital Bible Society, TechSoup, and regional ministry partners\n How a deployment comes together\n Assess the setting Document the local language, device types, power reliability, internet access, and whether use will be individual or group-based. Every other decision flows from this.\n Pick the access path Match the tool to the setting — phone transfer for simple one-to-one sharing, Raspberry Pi for group library access, projector for teaching sessions, satellite for truly remote deployments.\n Prepare the kit Load content, label cables, charge batteries, test each component, and pack the matching printed guides before the team departs.\n Train the local user Identify the person who will run the process after the visiting team leaves. Teach them, watch them do it independently, then confirm they can teach someone else.\n Support and improve Collect feedback through partner reports and field updates. Use it to improve content, equipment choices, and training materials for the next deployment.\n Every program is designed to leave something behind — not just a device, but a local person who knows how to use it and teach it forward.\n Download the Programs and Services overview as a printable PDF for board meetings or partner introductions.\n Download Programs PDF\n Key points\n VillageServer is a set of access paths, not a single product — combine only what the field needs\n Every deployment ends with a trained local person who can run it without the original team\n Printed guides keep the process going after the team leaves\n Download PDF\n Printable and shareable offline.\n Phone-Based Distribution\n Raspberry Pi System\n Power Systems and Solar\n Projector and Audio\n Questions? Need more info\n Eric's companion site has extended resources, setup instructions, and more — including the VillageServer OS download and additional field photos and testimonials.\n Visit the companion site →\n © 2026 VillageServer Initiative · Directory · Pamphlets · Built by ArkiTech-Sol\n\n---\n\n# Page: Projector Systems and Audio Solutions (projector-media.html)\n\nProjector Systems and Audio Solutions | VillageServer Initiative\n Skip to content\n VillageServer Initiative\n Back to directory\n Projector Systems and Audio Solutions\n One library, an entire room\n A projector changes the scale of what one library can do — a saved film, a teaching session, or a children's lesson projected onto a wall can reach a whole church, school, or evening gathering at once.\n Individual phone access is powerful. But there are moments when the whole community needs to be in the same space, watching or listening together. A projector turns one device's library into a shared experience for dozens of people at once.\n A portable projector connected to a VillageServer device for a group teaching session.\n When to use it\n Not every session calls for a projector. For one-on-one phone sharing or small group access, a projector is extra equipment to set up, carry, and protect. But for these situations, it's the right tool:\n Gospel film nights — a large screen and clear audio makes the experience communal and memorable\n Church or worship gatherings — song lyrics, Scripture passages, and teaching slides projected for the group\n Children's lessons — illustrated content and animated videos that a room of children can watch together\n Training sessions — visual walkthrough of the VillageServer setup steps for a group of new local leaders\n Evening outreach — an outdoor setting after dark where the whole community can gather around a screen\n Running a projector session\n Find the right space A shaded wall, classroom, or dark outdoor area. Avoid direct sunlight on the screen — even a partially lit screen is hard to watch.\n Connect power to everything first Projector, speaker, and source device all need reliable power before anyone sits down. Confirm all three are on before inviting the group.\n Connect the source device Use a phone, tablet, or laptop connected to the VillageServer library. Use an HDMI cable with the right adapter for the device.\n Test audio before testing video Sound quality matters more than screen size. A bad speaker ruins a film session more than a dim image does. Start with audio.\n Run a short test first Play a 2–3 minute clip before the full program. Confirm the image is focused, the audio reaches the back of the room, and the source device isn't locking its screen.\n Assign roles One person on audio, one on the source device, one managing the room. Don't let the person running the projector also try to manage the crowd.\n Common problems and quick fixes\n No image on screen — check the HDMI or adapter connection; try unplugging and replugging\n Sound from wrong source — set the Bluetooth speaker as the active audio output in phone settings before starting\n Screen too dim — move to a darker space rather than adjusting projector brightness; ambient light is the problem\n Cable doesn't fit the phone — carry both a USB-C to HDMI adapter and a 3.5mm audio cable to cover most devices\n Screen locks mid-session — disable auto-lock in display settings before the session starts\n Download the Projector and Audio overview as a printable PDF for kit planning or field briefings.\n Download Projector and Audio PDF\n Key points\n Test audio before video — sound matters more than screen size\n Disable screen auto-lock on the source device before the session\n One person per role: audio, device, room — don't combine\n Download PDF\n Printable and shareable offline.\n Power Systems and Solar\n Content Libraries\n Setup Guide\n Field Configurations\n Questions? Need more info\n Eric's companion site has extended resources, setup instructions, and more — including the VillageServer OS download and additional field photos and testimonials.\n Visit the companion site →\n © 2026 VillageServer Initiative · Directory · Pamphlets · Built by ArkiTech-Sol\n\n---\n\n# Page: Raspberry Pi VillageServer — Overview & Field Guide (raspberry-pi.html)\n\nRaspberry Pi VillageServer — Overview & Field Guide | VillageServer Initiative\n Skip to content\n VillageServer Initiative\n Back to directory\n Technology · Raspberry Pi VillageServer\n A library that fits in your hand\n The Raspberry Pi broadcasts a local Wi-Fi hotspot so nearby phones can browse an offline Bible library — no internet, no data plan, and no app install. Power it on, join VillageServer , enter village123 , open 10.42.0.1 , and the whole library is right there.\n Contents ▾\n On this page\n Overview & hardware\n Quick start guide\n What it looks like\n Using it in the field\n Troubleshooting\n Quick reference\n Wi-Fi name VillageServer\n Password village123\n Library URL 10.42.0.1\n Overview\n What is the VillageServer Pi?\n A Raspberry Pi running a local web server. Plug in microSD cards loaded with language libraries, power it on, and it broadcasts a Wi-Fi network. Anyone within range can join that network and open the library in a phone browser — like having a small offline internet made entirely of Scripture and gospel resources.\n The complete kit: carry case, Raspberry Pi, power bank, and USB readers holding the language library cards.\n What the Pi provides\n Auto-broadcasting Wi-Fi hotspot — turns on the moment the Pi powers up, no router or internet needed\n An offline library server — reachable at 10.42.0.1 from any phone browser, no app to install\n MicroSD-based language libraries — the language and resources available are determined by the cards plugged into USB readers\n Four content sections per language — Bible, Audio, Video, and Books, browsable and downloadable straight from the phone\n Fully offline operation — works indefinitely with no internet connection, anywhere there's power\n Hardware\n Item Details\n Raspberry Pi Pi 4 (2 GB+ RAM) or Pi 3B+\n System microSD 16–32 GB for the Pi OS\n Language cards One microSD per language, in USB readers\n Power supply 5V USB-C (Pi 4) or Micro-B (Pi 3) · power bank works\n Carry case Optional — keeps everything together for field use\n Get started\n Quick start & printable guides\n Download the printable handouts below — written for the person in the field, not an engineer. Save them to your phone before you lose internet access.\n Download Quick Start Guide\n Raspberry Pi System Overview\n See all printable guides →\n Content packs are loaded onto microSD cards before deployment. Once the Pi starts broadcasting, users need no Google Drive access, no internet, and no account — just the Wi-Fi password and the library address.\n The interface\n What you see on a phone\n When a phone joins the VillageServer Wi-Fi and opens 10.42.0.1 in a browser, this is what appears — a clean offline library home. No login, no app install, no data required. Try the demo — tap a language, then a section, then a file.\n 9:41 📶 🔋\n 10.42.0.1\n VillageServer Library\n English Tap to open →\n Español Tap to open →\n हिन्दी Tap to open →\n Français Tap to open →\n Português Tap to open →\n Kiswahili Tap to open →\n 中文 Tap to open →\n नेपाली Tap to open →\n ‹ Back\n English Library\n 📖 Bible\n 🎵 Audio\n 🎬 Video\n 📚 Books\n ‹ Back\n Bible\n Download complete — saved to Files\n Live click-through demo — tap your way through it.\n Bible — full Scripture text, chapter by chapter.\n Audio — dramatised audio Bible recordings, playable in the browser.\n Video — LUMO gospel films and other gospel media.\n Books — commentaries, discipleship materials, and reference PDFs.\n Updating Libraries: when a new microSD card is first inserted, the interface may briefly show \"Updating Libraries — please wait\" while the Pi reads it. Existing content stays available.\n For the field\n Using the library — step by step\n This is all a person in the field needs to know.\n 1\n Power on the VillageServer\n Plug in the power bank or power supply. Wait about 60 seconds for the Pi to boot. The Wi-Fi network appears automatically.\n 2\n Join the Wi-Fi network\n Open Settings → Wi-Fi and select VillageServer . Password: village123 .\n Note: your phone may warn \"no internet\" — that's expected. Tap \"Stay connected.\"\n 3\n Open the library\n Open any browser and type 10.42.0.1 in the address bar. The library home loads instantly.\n 4\n Choose a language and browse\n Tap a language card, then tap Bible, Audio, Video, or Books. Read, listen, or watch without saving anything first.\n 5\n Download to share later\n Hold any file link to save it. Share to other phones via AirDrop, Quick Share, LocalSend, Bluetooth, or USB — no Wi-Fi needed.\n Troubleshooting\n Common issues\n Phone joins Wi-Fi but the page won't load\n Double-check the address bar shows exactly 10.42.0.1 — not a search. Some phones auto-correct it into Google instead of visiting the address. Retype it manually.\n Phone warns \"no internet connection\"\n That's expected — the VillageServer network is intentionally offline. Tap \"Stay connected\" or \"Use anyway\" and continue.\n Wi-Fi network isn't showing up\n Give the Pi a full 60 seconds after power-on. If it still doesn't appear, power-cycle it (unplug, wait 10 seconds, plug back in).\n A language is missing\n The microSD card for that language may not be seated. Check that the USB reader is fully plugged in and power-cycle the Pi.\n Download the printable system overview to keep with the kit for field briefings.\n Download Raspberry Pi PDF\n Have more questions? Eric's companion site has extended resources, additional setup instructions, and more.\n Visit the companion site →\n © 2026 VillageServer Initiative · Directory · Pamphlets · Built by ArkiTech-Sol\n\n---\n\n# Page: Rollout and Reach (rollout.html)\n\nRollout and Reach | VillageServer Initiative\n Skip to content\n VillageServer Initiative\n Back to directory\n Rollout and Reach\n Prove it works locally before scaling it broadly\n A good rollout begins with one pilot site, one trained local leader, honest feedback, and simple honest reach reporting. Don't multiply kits until the first one works in real hands without the original team present.\n Scale creates problems you can't fix remotely. A training process that breaks down without the original team present will break down in twenty communities, not just one. The goal of a pilot is to prove that a local person can run the system alone — and teach it to the next person without help.\n Mapping deployment reach — each location represents a pilot or active deployment, not a projection.\n What a good pilot looks like\n A pilot isn't a soft launch or a demo. It's a real-world test that surfaces every problem before they become problems in ten other places. A pilot succeeds when the local lead can run the session without guessing and teach someone else without being prompted.\n One trusted site — a partner location where honest feedback is guaranteed, not a site chosen to look good\n One trained local lead — a specific person who can unpack, power on, connect, share, and teach the process independently\n Printed and digital handouts — left with the kit so the process continues after the team leaves\n Documented blockers — power failures, language gaps, device incompatibilities, content that didn't work — written down, not just noted verbally\n How to roll out carefully\n Choose the pilot site for honesty, not appearances Pick the location where a trusted partner will tell you what's broken, not what you want to hear. A site that reports zero problems is a site that isn't reporting honestly.\n Train one local lead completely Make sure one person can unpack, power on, connect, browse the library, share a resource, and explain the whole process to someone else — all without you in the room.\n Leave the PDF handouts with the kit Printed guides and digital copies on the device itself are what keep the process going after the visiting team leaves. Don't pack them with the team.\n Measure simple, honest reach Track the numbers that are actually verifiable: people trained, devices connected, files shared, groups served. Don't round up or project forward — report what actually happened.\n Document every blocker Write down power problems, language mismatches, device incompatibilities, content that didn't open, and steps that confused people. These are the improvements that make the second pilot better.\n Fix problems before scaling Don't send a second kit to a new location until the first pilot process is clean enough that a local leader can run it without corrections. Scale multiplies what exists — good and bad.\n Reach is credible when it is reported simply and honestly. One local leader who can teach the next person is more valuable than fifty kits sitting in a storeroom.\n Download the Rollout and Reach guide as a printable PDF for board presentations and field planning.\n Download Rollout and Reach PDF\n Key points\n A pilot succeeds when the local lead can run the process alone and teach the next person without prompting\n Report honest numbers — people trained, devices reached, groups served — not projections\n Fix every documented problem before multiplying kits to new locations\n Download PDF\n Printable and shareable offline.\n Full Initiative Overview\n Testimonials\n Photos and Visuals\n Field FAQ\n Questions? Need more info\n Eric's companion site has extended resources, setup instructions, and more — including the VillageServer OS download and additional field photos and testimonials.\n Visit the companion site →\n © 2026 VillageServer Initiative · Directory · Pamphlets · Built by ArkiTech-Sol\n\n---\n\n# Page: Satellite Systems and Content Acquisition (satellite.html)\n\nSatellite Systems and Content Acquisition | VillageServer Initiative\n Skip to content\n VillageServer Initiative\n Back to directory\n Satellite Systems and Content Acquisition\n Receive broadcasts. Replay locally.\n The satellite piece repurposes unused dishes to receive Free-to-Air Christian radio or video already broadcasting over a region, then plays or records it for local ministry use.\n This satellite path is not a remote-control system for VillageServer. It is a receive-and-replay broadcast path: a repurposed dish, a generic LNB, a receiver or recorder, and a local screen, wall, speaker, or projector.\n A repurposed satellite dish retrofitted with a generic LNB for Free-to-Air broadcast reception.\n What the satellite part really does\n First, an unused satellite dish is repurposed. The team retrofits it with a generic LNB, aims it at a satellite that covers the region, and receives Christian radio or video content that is already being broadcast Free-to-Air.\n The stream can be played live through a TV, projector, speaker, wall, or screen in a home, church, school, or community center. With an HTML recorder or similar recorder, a pastor or local leader can save programs and replay them later as often as needed.\n What it is not\n Not a Raspberry Pi update link — it does not currently update the VillageServer live unless a recorded stream is later copied to microSD and loaded into the library.\n Not remote team coordination — unless custom uplink work is added through a teleport organization, this is not how the field team sends messages back.\n Not the emergency-comms plan — amateur radio may become a future path, but it is separate from this satellite receive model.\n Not required for the core kit — the offline VillageServer library should still work without the dish.\n Checking satellite hardware alignment during the VillageServer field test run.\n How a field setup works\n Repurpose an unused dish Find a dish that's already mounted and aimed at something — most owners are happy to let it stay up and simply not be used, rather than having it taken down.\n Swap in a generic LNB Remove the dish's existing proprietary LNB (the part at the dish's focal point that downconverts the ~12GHz satellite signal to the ~1GHz frequency a receiver can use) and replace it with a generic, universal LNB.\n Connect a satellite finder Run a coax cable from the new LNB to a satellite finder/meter. This gives a live signal reading while the dish is being moved, so you know when you're on target.\n Look up the satellite on LyngSat.com LyngSat.com lists the satellites broadcasting over a given region and the technical details for each Free-to-Air Christian station: the transponder's frequency, polarization, and symbol rate.\n Get the aiming numbers from a Satfinder app A satellite-finder app (such as Satfinder) converts your location and the chosen satellite into the skew angle, azimuth, and elevation needed to physically point the dish. Both the LyngSat numbers and the Satfinder numbers are needed before the dish can be aimed.\n Aim the dish and confirm lock Adjust azimuth, elevation, and skew while watching the satellite finder for a signal lock on the correct transponder.\n Play the content locally Send the radio or video to a speaker, TV, projector, wall, or screen where the group can listen or watch together.\n Record useful programs With an HTML recorder or similar recorder, save broadcasts so the local leader can replay them later without waiting for the live signal.\n Bridge to VillageServer only if needed If a saved program should live in the local library, copy the recorded file to microSD and load it through the Raspberry Pi VillageServer.\n Two tools, two different jobs: LyngSat.com tells you what you're aiming for — frequency, polarization, and symbol rate for the satellite and station. The Satfinder app tells you how to aim — skew angle, azimuth, and elevation for your location. You need numbers from both before the dish can be pointed correctly.\n As an example of what this looks like in practice: at his home, Eric points a dish at Galaxy 19, tuned to a transponder at 12.053GHz, vertically polarized. The exact satellite, frequency, and polarization will be different for every region — always confirm the current numbers on LyngSat.com rather than reusing this example.\n Think of satellite as a regional content receiver, not the heart of the system. It is completely separate from the Raspberry Pi VillageServer — the dish, LNB, finder, and recorder are their own self-contained setup, and the VillageServer library remains local, offline-first, and useful without a live signal.\n Questions about a specific satellite setup, LNB swap, or aiming problem in the field? Email villageserverinitiative@gmail.com and the team can help troubleshoot.\n Satellite aiming calculator\n Elevation · Azimuth · LNB skew · IF frequency · beam map\n ›\n Enter your location and pick a satellite to get exact aiming angles for your dish, the IF frequency to tune your receiver to, and a signal quality estimate based on how high the satellite sits in your sky.\n Your location\n Locate me\n Latitude: −90 (S) to 90 (N) · Longitude: −180 (W) to 180 (E)\n Target satellite\n — select a satellite —\n Downlink frequency (MHz) from LyngSat.com\n LNB type\n Universal Ku low band — LO 9750 MHz (10.7–11.7 GHz)\n Universal Ku high band — LO 10600 MHz (11.7–12.75 GHz)\n Fixed Ku — LO 10750 MHz\n C-band standard — LO 5150 MHz (3.7–4.2 GHz)\n Enter LO manually…\n Calculate\n —\n Elevation\n —\n Azimuth\n —\n LNB skew\n —\n IF frequency\n Signal quality estimate\n Map shows the satellite's sub-satellite point (blue) and approximate beam footprint. The dashed ellipse is a rough coverage guide — actual signal strength varies by dish size and the satellite's EIRP contour for your location. Elevation angle is the primary signal quality predictor: above 20° is generally workable; below 10° is marginal.\n Download the corrected Satellite Systems overview as a printable PDF for kit planning and field briefings.\n Download Satellite Systems PDF\n Key points\n Receives Free-to-Air Christian radio or video already broadcasting over the region\n Use LyngSat.com for frequency, polarization, and symbol rate\n Use a Satfinder app for skew angle, azimuth, and elevation\n Can project or play the stream live for a group\n Can record programs for later replay with a dedicated recorder\n Completely separate from the Raspberry Pi VillageServer\n Download PDF\n Printable and shareable offline.\n Power Systems and Solar\n Raspberry Pi System\n Content Libraries\n Field Configurations\n Questions? Need more info\n Eric's companion site has extended resources, setup instructions, and more — including the VillageServer OS download and additional field photos and testimonials.\n Visit the companion site →\n © 2026 VillageServer Initiative · Directory · Pamphlets · Built by ArkiTech-Sol\n\n---\n\n# Page: Sharing the Library (sharing-library.html)\n\nSharing the Library | VillageServer Initiative\n Skip to content\n VillageServer Initiative\n Back to directory\n Sharing the Library\n One person receives, then teaches the next\n The library becomes powerful when it moves — when one person saves a useful resource and passes it to someone nearby, and that person does the same. Sharing turns a kit into a multiplying access path.\n The goal is not just access. The goal is repeatable sharing. When the first person receives a resource, saves it, and teaches the next person without needing help, the library has done its job — and it will keep doing it long after the team has left.\n Library sharing in action — one person passes a resource to the next, no data required.\n What makes sharing work\n Three things break sharing chains: picking the wrong resource to start with, using a method that's too complicated to remember, and not confirming the new person can repeat it. Getting all three right is the whole task.\n Start with one file, not the whole library — the most useful resource right now; Scripture chapter, audio Bible, or a film that the community has been asking about\n Choose a patient, teachable local person — someone who will repeat the process and teach others, not just someone who is nearby\n Avoid complicated folders and unexplained apps — if they have to figure out why it's in that folder, the chain breaks at the next handoff\n Leave a printed step guide — the handout is what keeps sharing going after the team leaves; it replaces you\n Making sure it keeps moving\n Connect or copy to the first device Use VillageServer Wi-Fi, microSD, USB, or phone sharing to get one resource onto the first device. Confirm it opens before going any further.\n Choose the one most useful file Pick the resource most likely to help someone immediately: a Bible chapter in the local language, an audio recording, a gospel film, or a training PDF. One file, clearly chosen.\n Save it where they can find it again Put it in a folder or app the person can locate without guidance — somewhere they already know, not a new folder with a technical name.\n Share it to one nearby person Use the simplest transfer method that works for the two phones in front of you. Watch the whole transfer and confirm the second person has the file.\n Have the new person share it again Ask the second person to share the same file to a third person right now, while you watch. This is the moment sharing becomes their skill, not just a thing you showed them.\n Leave the printed handout The PDF walks through the same steps in plain language. Keep one with the kit and give one to the person who will keep teaching it forward.\n Sharing turns a kit into a multiplying access path. The visiting team is just the first link in the chain.\n Download the Sharing the Library guide as a printable PDF for field training and kit handouts.\n Download Sharing the Library PDF\n Key points\n Start with one useful resource — not the whole library — and make sure it's saved and findable\n The process works when the new person shares it to someone else without being prompted\n A printed handout keeps the chain going after the team leaves\n Download PDF\n Printable and shareable offline.\n Phone-Based Distribution\n Transfer Between Devices\n Get and Share Resources\n Printable Pamphlets\n Questions? Need more info\n Eric's companion site has extended resources, setup instructions, and more — including the VillageServer OS download and additional field photos and testimonials.\n Visit the companion site →\n © 2026 VillageServer Initiative · Directory · Pamphlets · Built by ArkiTech-Sol\n\n---\n\n# Page: VillageServer Initiative (spring-uganda-run.html)\n\nVillageServer Initiative This campaign page has moved to the VillageServer Initiative directory .\n\n---\n\n# Page: Testimonials (testimonials.html)\n\nTestimonials | VillageServer Initiative\n Skip to content\n VillageServer Initiative\n Back to directory\n Testimonials\n Stories make the technology human\n Real testimonies from missionaries and local leaders — short, honest, and tied to a specific outcome. What changed because the resources were available offline?\n A useful testimony doesn't need to sound polished. It needs to say what changed, who was helped, and what should happen next. The goal is a story that a board member or potential partner can understand in thirty seconds — and remember after the meeting is over.\n Community members reading together — the kind of moment that makes a testimony worth telling.\n What makes a testimony worth using\n Good testimonies are specific, short, and connected to something real. The technology is a means — the story is about what the technology made possible for a person or community.\n Specific outcome — not \"it was helpful\" but \"for the first time, the pastor had a Bible in his own language on his phone, saved offline\"\n Clear moment — a training session, a film night, a local leader successfully teaching someone else the setup process\n Honest numbers — how many people were trained, how many devices received resources, how many groups were served\n What should happen next — every good story points toward a next step, whether that's more kits, better content, or a new training partner\n Resources reaching a new community — the moment behind every good testimony.\n How to collect and use testimonies\n Ask one clear question What changed because these resources were available offline? Simple, open, and focused on the ministry outcome — not the technology.\n Get permission before publishing Confirm whether the name, photo, location, and quote can be shared publicly. Use general location language when specific details could create risk for the person or community.\n Keep it short Two or three sentences that a board member can understand and repeat. If it takes a paragraph to explain the context, the testimony isn't ready to use yet.\n Tie the story to an access path Connect the outcome to phone sharing, a local library, a projector teaching session, or field training — so the reader knows which part of the program created the result.\n Protect sensitive places Use \"a remote village in East Africa\" instead of specific names or locations when public details could create safety concerns for the people or local leaders involved.\n A good photo shows people learning, setting up, or sharing resources — not close-ups of hardware. The story is about the people.\n Download the Testimonials guide as a printable PDF for collecting field stories and preparing board presentations.\n Download Testimonials PDF\n Key points\n A useful testimony is specific, short, and tied to a real outcome — not just general praise\n Always confirm permission before using names, photos, or locations\n The best photo shows people learning or sharing — not equipment\n Download PDF\n Printable and shareable offline.\n Photos and Visuals\n Rollout and Reach\n Mission Statement\n Ministry Resource Partners\n Questions? Need more info\n Eric's companion site has extended resources, setup instructions, and more — including the VillageServer OS download and additional field photos and testimonials.\n Visit the companion site →\n © 2026 VillageServer Initiative · Directory · Pamphlets · Built by ArkiTech-Sol\n\n---\n\n# Page: Transfer Resources Between Devices (transfer.html)\n\nTransfer Resources Between Devices | VillageServer Initiative\n Skip to content\n VillageServer Initiative\n Back to directory\n Transfer Resources Between Devices\n One route, one test file, then confirm it opens\n Every transfer works best when you pick one route for the two devices in front of you, send one small file first, and confirm the receiving person can open it and find it again without help.\n The most common transfer mistake is choosing the wrong method for the devices in the room, or sending too much too fast. Choose one route that matches the actual devices, test with one file, and only move the full library once you know the connection is solid.\n A field team preparing resources for transfer — matching the method to the devices in front of them.\n Match the route to the devices\n iPhone to iPhone — AirDrop when both are nearby and Bluetooth/Wi-Fi are on; confirm receiving is set to \"Everyone\" before starting\n Android to Android — Quick Share when available, or LocalSend when both phones are on the same local network\n iPhone and Android mixed — LocalSend on a shared local network, USB through a computer, or a microSD card with an adapter\n Computer to phone — use Finder (Mac) or Windows File Explorer via USB cable; for Android, enable file transfer mode when the cable connects\n microSD to phone — insert the card, open Files or a file manager, copy to internal storage, then confirm the file opens before removing the card\n Running the transfer step by step\n Identify both devices Ask what each device is: iPhone, Android, Windows computer, Mac, USB drive, or microSD card. Getting this wrong means choosing the wrong method.\n Pick the matching route Use the list above to select the right method. Use AirDrop, Quick Share, LocalSend, USB cable, or card reader — whichever fits these two specific devices.\n Send one small file first Test with one PDF or audio file before sending a large folder. A test file reveals connection problems before wasting time on a large transfer that fails halfway.\n Confirm where the file landed Show the receiving person exactly where the file went: Downloads, Files, Photos, or a specific folder. If they can't find it, it won't get used.\n Open it offline Turn off mobile data or enable airplane mode if safe, then confirm the file still opens without a connection. This is the moment that proves the transfer worked.\n Let the receiver do the second transfer Have the receiving person do the next transfer to a third device while you watch. This is how the method becomes something they own, not something they were shown.\n Device-specific PDF guides\n Each printable PDF covers one specific route with step-by-step instructions, screenshots described in plain language, and a troubleshooting section. Download and print the ones that match your setting before you leave internet access.\n iPhone to iPhone PDF\n Android to Android PDF\n iPhone to Android PDF\n Android to iPhone PDF\n Computer to Phone PDF\n microSD to Phone PDF\n Download the full Transfer overview as a printable PDF for kit packs and field briefings.\n Download Transfer Overview PDF\n Key points\n Identify both device types before choosing a transfer method — don't guess\n Always send one small test file before moving the full library\n Confirm the file opens offline and the receiver knows where it's saved\n Download PDF\n Printable and shareable offline.\n Phone-Based Distribution\n Get and Share Resources\n Sharing the Library\n Printable Pamphlets\n Questions? Need more info\n Eric's companion site has extended resources, setup instructions, and more — including the VillageServer OS download and additional field photos and testimonials.\n Visit the companion site →\n © 2026 VillageServer Initiative · Directory · Pamphlets · Built by ArkiTech-Sol\n\n---\n\n# Page: Photos and Visuals (visuals.html)\n\nPhotos and Visuals | VillageServer Initiative\n Skip to content\n VillageServer Initiative\n Back to directory\n Photos and Visuals\n A good visual answers one question at a glance\n Photos, diagrams, and short videos help non-technical viewers understand the field model quickly. The goal is fewer, stronger images — each one showing people using the kit in a real setting, not hardware sitting on a table.\n Most people who see VillageServer for the first time aren't technical. They need to understand what the initiative does before they can decide to support it. A good photo communicates in three seconds what three paragraphs struggle to say: what is being used, who is using it, and why it matters.\n Field readiness workshop — testing kit components before a real deployment, with real people in the frame.\n What makes a visual work\n The strongest images are candid, not staged. They show a real moment — a setup being tested, a local leader explaining the process, a group watching a film projected on a wall. Equipment-only photos don't carry the weight that a photo of people does.\n Best photo — a person using the kit in a real setting: a local leader connecting a phone, a group gathered around a projected screen, a missionary handing someone a printed guide\n Best diagram — a simple flow showing power → server → phone → sharing; non-technical viewers should be able to trace the path without explanation\n Best short video — a setup sequence that shows the whole process from unpacking to first file, demonstrating confidence and practicality in under two minutes\n Phones connecting during a test run — the moment the library becomes accessible.\n Satellite alignment check — documenting the optional components in context.\n How to capture and use visuals well\n Show the people, not just the equipment Photos of a Raspberry Pi on a table or cables in a case don't communicate the mission. Photos of a group using the kit in a real setting do.\n Capture real setup and handoff moments The moments that matter most visually are: powering on the kit for the first time, a local leader being trained, a group receiving resources, and a successful file transfer.\n Write plain captions One sentence explaining the ministry purpose. \"A local leader connecting a phone during the first session\" is better than \"Setup testing in progress.\"\n Use fewer, stronger images Five well-chosen photos tell a better story than thirty random ones. A page that feels like a file dump doesn't hold anyone's attention.\n Keep permissions documented Only publish images and video that are approved for public ministry use. Keep a simple record of who approved what, especially for photos of identifiable people in sensitive regions.\n Download the Photos and Visuals guide as a printable PDF for presentation planning and board packets.\n Download Visuals Guide PDF\n Key points\n Photos of people using the kit communicate more than photos of equipment alone\n Use fewer, stronger images — a curated selection beats a large dump of files\n Keep permissions documented for any identifiable people, especially in sensitive regions\n Download PDF\n Printable and shareable offline.\n Testimonials\n Full Initiative Overview\n Setup Guide\n Printable Pamphlets\n Questions? Need more info\n Eric's companion site has extended resources, setup instructions, and more — including the VillageServer OS download and additional field photos and testimonials.\n Visit the companion site →\n © 2026 VillageServer Initiative · Directory · Pamphlets · Built by ArkiTech-Sol\n\n---\n\n# Source: companion.md\n\n# Eric's Companion Site\n\nEric maintains a companion site with extended resources, setup instructions, the\nVillageServer OS download, and additional field photos and testimonials:\nhttps://sites.google.com/view/villageserver-initiative\n\nTo teach the assistant about the companion site's content, paste the relevant\ntext from that site below this line (headings and paragraphs are fine — the\nassistant reads plain text). Replace this note when you add real content.";
